<mujoco model="pololu_calibrated">
  <!--
    Hidden plant for the MuJoCo deployment studies. REBUILT 2026-08-20 from the
    manufacturer's drawing plus measurements on the actual robot; the previous
    version (git history, up to 2026-08-19) was a plausible stand-in whose
    chassis size, wheel width, caster size, ground clearance, mass and CoM were
    all guesses that had been tuned against symptoms rather than measured.

    THIS FILE IS THE HIDDEN GROUND TRUTH. The active-learning pipeline must
    never read it: it only ever sees ROBOTCFG.CFG / robot_config.yaml, exactly
    like the real robot.

    models/pololu_hidden.yaml IS AUTHORITATIVE for every value it lists - the
    motor, wheel geometry, frictions, encoder, IMU and mocap. wmr_simulator's
    build_plant_xml patches them into this file before compiling it, so editing
    an attribute here that the yaml also carries changes nothing. Change it in
    the yaml; the numbers below are kept in step so this file reads true on its
    own. Masses, chassis geometry and contact softness live here only.

    ================================ SOURCES =================================

    (A) models/3pi-plus-chassis-kit-dimensions.pdf, the Pololu drawing for item
        3725, and the STEP files beside it. The drawing is 1:1 vector art, so
        the numbers below were not scaled off a picture: a least-squares circle
        fit to the caster arc in the side views of BOTH page 1 and page 2 agrees
        to 0.02 mm on centre and radius (r = 6.35 mm, residual 0.027 mm).
        Directly read off it:
            body outer diameter        97.0 mm  (bumper skirt; chassis 96.0)
            wheel diameter             32.0 mm
            caster ball diameter       12.7 mm
            ground clearance            5.0 mm  (with skirt; 5.8 without)
            overall height             36.1 mm
            chassis tub length/height  77.9 / 15.0 mm
        Fitted from the same vector art, relative to the wheel axle:
            caster ball centre         40.37 mm behind, 6.06 mm above ground

    (B) Measured on the robot (2026-08-19): total mass 171 g with batteries;
        CoM 2-3 mm behind the drive axle; at rest it sits on the caster ball and
        is very nearly level, nose a shade up; the nose dives onto the floor
        under braking with roughly 5 mm of travel from rest, and that contact
        is a low-friction point graze, not a grab.

    (C) Caliper on the hardware: wheel track 84.2 mm, and it is consistent with
        the drawing rather than in conflict with it. The 96.0 mm across the top
        view is the span over the MOTOR SHAFT ENDS, not the tires: the wheels
        are pressed on inboard of the shaft ends, and half a wheel width comes
        off each side on top of that. Nothing here needs the drawing's number -
        84.2 mm is a direct measurement of the thing the model wants.

    ======================== WHY THE REST IS LEVEL-ISH =======================

    The resting attitude is not a free parameter, it is contact geometry: a
    LEVEL robot needs the ball centre 16.0 - 6.35 = 9.65 mm below the axle, and
    every 0.1 mm of departure from that is 0.14 deg of pitch on the 40.37 mm
    lever. So the drawing and observation (B) can be checked against each other,
    and they very nearly agree - they disagree by 0.54 mm, i.e. by 0.8 deg.

    The drawing puts the ball centre 9.94 mm down, i.e. hanging 0.29 mm BELOW
    the wheel contact plane, which rests the robot nose-DOWN by 0.41 deg
    (verified in the compiled model: the nose skid ends up 0.43 mm below its
    level height). (B) says nose a shade UP. This file uses 9.40 mm, which rests
    it nose-up by 0.61 deg (0.35 from the geometry, the rest from the wheels
    penetrating deeper than the lightly-loaded ball), and takes (B) over the
    drawing for two reasons: it
    is a direct observation of the actual robot, and the discrepancy has an
    obvious physical home. The drawing shows the ball FREE-HANGING at the bottom
    of its socket; on the floor it is pushed up into the socket and takes up
    that clearance, and a few tenths of a millimetre of slop in a ball caster is
    entirely ordinary.

    This is the ONE number here fitted to an observation rather than read off
    the drawing, so it is called out. To go back to the drawing exactly, set the
    caster body's z to -0.00994 and expect a nose-down rest.

    Either way the CoM must be BEHIND the axle, or the caster is not loaded at
    all and the robot rocks on two contacts. It is, by 2.5 mm, which puts
    2.5/40.37 = 6.2% of the weight on the ball - matched by the solver to the
    digit.

    =============================== CONTACTS =================================

    Four contact geoms, and only four: two wheels, the caster ball, the nose
    skid. Everything else - the CAD meshes, the mass primitives - is contype=0.
    Modelling the contact set explicitly is the point: with a colliding chassis
    cylinder the front contact was its RIM, a knife edge whose friction MuJoCo
    silently took as max(floor, geom default) = 1.0, higher than the wheels.

    THE WHEEL COLLISION CYLINDER IS NARROWER THAN THE TIRE (3.0 mm against
    7.5 mm), deliberately. MuJoCo reduces a cylinder on a plane to a two-point
    LINE contact at its two rim edges, both at full Coulomb friction, and those
    two points fight every turn: the cylinder's half-length IS the scrub lever.
    The real tire is crowned, so a 7 mm cylinder models a flat-tread tire the
    robot does not have. Open loop at duty 0.35 with a 0.05 split, fraction of
    the commanded wheel-speed split achieved: 7 mm 24%, 3.8 mm 79%, 3.0 mm 92%.
    The yaml carries the full profile measurement and why 3.0 was picked.

    That fixed the turning, but it did NOT reduce what the motor has to deliver:
    at the datasheet stall torque, circle_medium diverges on 6 of 6 seeds at
    3.8 mm exactly as it did at 7 mm. Worth knowing, because "the sim needs more
    torque than the datasheet" and "MuJoCo invents scrub" are the two obvious
    explanations for the same symptom and only one of them was true.

    The nose skid is a 3 mm sphere at the front lip, which is the "nice point
    collision" in (B) - a moulded plastic lip is a rounded edge, and a sphere is
    both the honest shape and the one a contact solver handles gracefully. Its
    lowest point sits at the drawing's 5.0 mm clearance, so it touches down at
    6.3 deg of nose-down pitch (the true lip at r = 48.5 mm would be 5.9 deg).

    ================================ MOTOR ===================================

    ctrl = duty * omega_max on a MuJoCo velocity actuator, which applies
    torque = kv * (ctrl - qvel): algebraically a DC motor's (kt/R)(V - ke w), so
    this is physically founded rather than a servo bolted on. kv and armature
    are derived in the yaml from omega_max, stall torque and the free wheel-speed
    time constant; joint damping is 0 because back-EMF is already the kv term.

    EMERGENT (not set here, and deliberately different from the nominal yaml):
    the effective rolling radius (contact penetration) and the effective
    wheelbase (tire scrub). Recovering those is the point of the identification
    benchmark; the current measured values live in mujoco_sim.truth and are
    pinned by its test. Re-measure them after ANY change to this file.
  -->
  <compiler angle="radian" inertiafromgeom="true"/>

  <option timestep="0.001" integrator="implicit" gravity="0 0 -9.81"/>

  <visual>
    <global azimuth="45" elevation="-30" offwidth="1920" offheight="1080"/>
    <headlight ambient="0.3 0.3 0.3" diffuse="0.6 0.6 0.6"/>
  </visual>

  <asset>
    <texture type="skybox" builtin="gradient" rgb1="0.3 0.5 0.7" rgb2="0 0 0" width="512" height="3072"/>
    <texture type="2d" name="groundplane" builtin="checker" mark="edge"
             rgb1="0.2 0.3 0.4" rgb2="0.1 0.2 0.3" markrgb="0.8 0.8 0.8"
             width="300" height="300"/>
    <material name="groundplane" texture="groundplane" texuniform="true" texrepeat="10 10" reflectance="0"/>
    <texture name="wheel_tex" type="2d" builtin="checker"
            rgb1="0.05 0.05 0.05" rgb2="0.9 0.9 0.9"
            width="256" height="256"/>
    <material name="wheel_mat" texture="wheel_tex"
              texrepeat="6 1" texuniform="true" reflectance="0.0"/>
    <!--
      Pololu's own STEP assembly, tessellated and split by
      scripts/convert_chassis_mesh.py (see it for the frame conversion and why
      the wheels had to be carved out of the chassis shell). Visual only: the
      collision set stays four analytic primitives. A faceted mesh rolling on a
      plane is polygonal rolling, which at 3.7 m/s puts a facet-rate ripple
      well above the 1 kHz physics step - the exact hopping artefact the
      contacts were tuned to remove.
    -->
    <mesh name="chassis_mesh" file="pololu_chassis.stl"/>
    <mesh name="wheel_mesh" file="pololu_wheel.stl"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <!--
      Rolled PVC sheet ~5 mm thick on a hard surface: for a 171 g robot this is
      effectively RIGID, and the hardware bears that out - nobody expects this
      robot to sink into that floor by more than a few hundredths of a
      millimetre. Contact penetration is not a free knob, it sets the effective
      rolling radius, which is the thing identification has to recover: 3.1 mm
      of penetration at a soft (0.015 5) collapsed the effective radius
      16.0 -> 12.9 mm. Check r_eff in mujoco_sim.truth after touching this.

      THE WHEEL GEOMS CARRY THE SAME solref/solimp, and they have to. With no
      explicit <pair>, MuJoCo MIXES the two geoms' solref rather than taking the
      stiffer - setting (0.003 2) on the floor alone gave a wheel contact of
      (0.0115 1.5), the average with the wheel geom's untouched default
      (0.02 1), and the resting penetration barely moved (0.083 -> 0.080 mm).
      Same trap as the elementwise-MAX friction rule, opposite direction: a
      value set on one side of a contact is quietly diluted by the other side's
      default. With both sides set the average is the value, and the resting
      penetration is 0.04 mm.

      The time constant cannot go below 2*timestep, so stiffness and timestep
      are coupled: (0.003 2) is 3x the 0.001 s step, which is why the step is
      0.001 and not 0.002. Physics is not the bottleneck in a deployment - the
      firmware port ticks at 100 Hz - so the halved step costs little.
    -->
    <geom name="floor" type="plane" size="0 0 0.05" material="groundplane"
          friction="0.9 0.005 0.0001"
          solref="0.003 2" solimp="0.99 0.9999 0.0005"/>

    <light pos="0 0 1.5" dir="0 0 -1" directional="true"/>

    <!--
      Body frame: origin at the WHEEL-AXLE MIDPOINT, +x forward, +z up. That is
      the pose the mocap and every pipeline consumer means by "the robot", which
      is why mocap.offset_xyz in the yaml is all zeros. Level, the floor is at
      z = -0.016 (the wheel radius), so a height above the floor h maps to
      z = h - 0.016.
    -->
    <body name="chassis" pos="0 0 0.0163">
      <freejoint name="root"/>
      <camera name="track_cam" mode="track" pos="0 -0.6 0.25" xyaxes="1 0 0 0 0.6 1"/>

      <!--
        MASS BUDGET. Total 171 g measured (B). The split is a decomposition of
        that measurement, not four independent guesses - it has to sum to 171 g
        and put the CoM where (B) says, and test_mujoco_plant asserts both.

          chassis_deck    74.24 g   tub + skirt + motors + clips (the remainder)
          control_board   32.0  g   3pi+ 32mm control board, on top of the tub
          battery_pack    52.0  g   4x AAA NiMH at ~13 g
          wheels        2x 5.63 g   density-derived, see the wheel geoms
          caster ball      1.5  g   12.7 mm POM sphere
                        ________
                         171.0  g

        CoM: 2.5 mm behind the axle, 15.0 mm above the floor. The longitudinal
        offset is the middle of the 2-3 mm in (B) - measured over that range,
        -2.0/-2.5/-3.0 mm are within noise of each other on every open-loop
        stability metric, so the midpoint is taken and nothing hangs on it. The
        HEIGHT is the weakest number in this file: it is not measured, it is
        inferred from the batteries being in the underside tub, and it is the
        lever arm for every pitch excursion. Measure it if the pitch behaviour
        ever needs to be trusted quantitatively.

        The deck and board are placed by geometry and the pack carries the
        offset that lands the total - which is also what (B) describes, one
        battery pair sitting further back than the other.
      -->
      <geom name="chassis_visual" type="mesh" mesh="chassis_mesh"
            contype="0" conaffinity="0" mass="0"
            rgba="0.93 0.93 0.93 1.0"/>

      <geom name="chassis_deck" type="cylinder"
            size="0.0485 0.0077" pos="0 0 -0.0033"
            contype="0" conaffinity="0"
            rgba="0.93 0.93 0.93 0.0" mass="0.0783"/>

      <geom name="control_board" type="cylinder"
            size="0.045 0.001" pos="0 0 0.006"
            contype="0" conaffinity="0"
            rgba="0.10 0.35 0.20 0.0" mass="0.032"/>

      <geom name="battery_pack" type="box"
            size="0.0225 0.020 0.0055" pos="-0.0070566 0 -0.00174"
            contype="0" conaffinity="0"
            rgba="0.2 0.2 0.2 0.0" mass="0.052"/>

      <!--
        32 x 7 mm wheels (A). The geoms declare no mass: MuJoCo's default
        density gives 5.63 g each, which is about right for a plastic rim and a
        rubber tire, and `_wheel_spin_inertia` in plant.py depends on that
        derivation to subtract the geom's share from the armature.
      -->
      <body name="left_wheel" pos="0.0 0.0421 0.0">
        <joint name="left_wheel_joint" type="hinge" axis="0 1 0"
               damping="0.0" armature="3.5205e-6" frictionloss="2e-6"/>
        <geom name="left_wheel_visual" type="mesh" mesh="wheel_mesh"
              contype="0" conaffinity="0" mass="0"
              rgba="0.15 0.15 0.15 1"/>
        <geom name="left_wheel_geom" type="cylinder" size="0.016 0.0019"
              rgba="1 1 1 0" euler="1.5708 0 0"
              material="wheel_mat" mass="0.0036"
              friction="0.9 0.001 0.0001"
              solref="0.003 2" solimp="0.99 0.9999 0.0005"/>
      </body>

      <body name="right_wheel" pos="0.0 -0.0421 0.0">
        <joint name="right_wheel_joint" type="hinge" axis="0 1 0"
               damping="0.0" armature="3.5205e-6" frictionloss="2e-6"/>
        <geom name="right_wheel_visual" type="mesh" mesh="wheel_mesh"
              contype="0" conaffinity="0" mass="0"
              rgba="0.15 0.15 0.15 1"/>
        <geom name="right_wheel_geom" type="cylinder" size="0.016 0.0019"
              rgba="1 1 1 0" euler="1.5708 0 0"
              material="wheel_mat" mass="0.0036"
              friction="0.9 0.001 0.0001"
              solref="0.003 2" solimp="0.99 0.9999 0.0005"/>
      </body>

      <!--
        ROLLING BALL CASTER. Radius and the 40.37 mm setback are the circle fit
        in (A); the 9.40 mm drop is (A)'s 9.94 mm corrected for socket seating
        clearance so the robot rests nose-up as observed - see "WHY THE REST IS
        LEVEL-ISH" above, the only place in this file where an observation
        overrides the drawing. The joint is a BALL joint - the ball rotates in its socket but
        does not translate relative to the chassis, which is what a caster is -
        and `frictionloss` is the socket drag, the only thing resisting it.

        Do not turn this back into a rigidly-fixed sphere. As a skid it dragged
        sideways at ~780 mm/s through every turn, and that parasitic drag was
        what the old 6x-oversized motor was silently paying for; with the
        datasheet motor and a skid, wheel speeds collapsed to 58/12 rad/s
        against a commanded 92/46 and broke into a 10 Hz limit cycle.
      -->
      <body name="caster" pos="-0.04037 0.0 -0.00940">
        <joint name="caster_ball" type="ball" frictionloss="2e-7" damping="0"/>
        <geom name="caster_geom" type="sphere" size="0.00635"
              rgba="0.5 0.5 0.5 1"
              friction="0.35 0.001 0.0001"
              mass="0.0015"/>
        <!--
          Visual only: a painted dot so the ball's rotation is readable. A
          smooth sphere on a smooth floor gives the eye nothing to track, and
          whether the caster is ROLLING or dragging is the one thing this body
          exists to get right (see the skid warning above) - the render camera
          can go under the floor and look straight up at it.

          It starts at the bottom of the ball, i.e. at the contact patch, which
          is where a camera below the floor sees it. contype/conaffinity 0 keeps
          the contact set at the four geoms declared above, and mass="0" keeps
          the ball's 1.5 g and its inertia exactly as measured - a marker that
          changed how the caster rolls would defeat its own purpose. It stands
          0.5 mm proud of the surface because a dot fully inside an opaque
          sphere cannot be seen at all, and a dot flush with it disappears at
          the grazing angles a chase camera spends most of a run at.
        -->
        <geom name="caster_marker" type="sphere" size="0.0017" pos="0 0 -0.0052"
              rgba="0.9 0.1 0.1 1" contype="0" conaffinity="0" mass="0"/>
      </body>

      <!--
        NOSE SKID. The front lip of the skirt, as a 3 mm sphere whose lowest
        point is the drawing's 5.0 mm ground clearance (z = -0.011). Massless:
        its mass is already inside chassis_deck.
      -->
      <geom name="nose_skid" type="sphere" size="0.003"
            pos="0.0455 0 -0.008"
            rgba="0.93 0.93 0.93 1.0"
            friction="0.35 0.001 0.0001"
            mass="0"/>

    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <!--
    Explicit pairs override MuJoCo's elementwise-MAXIMUM friction combination,
    which is the whole reason this block exists: without a pair, a geom meant to
    be slippery silently inherits the floor's mu. Both of these are surfaces
    that must slide, and both are reached across a gap at speed, so both get
    explicit stiffness/damping instead of the floor's solref.

    solref="-k -c" is explicit (stiffness N/m, damping N-s/m). The two contacts
    get very different values because they do different jobs.

    The CASTER carries static load continuously, so it is soft and well damped:
    k = 300 N/m against the 0.171 kg chassis is zeta ~ 0.6. It was -3
    (zeta ~ 0.2) until 2026-08-18, which left it badly underdamped - a 1 deg
    peak-to-peak pitch limit cycle at 5.6 Hz standing still, and -13 deg at
    7.1 Hz driving circle_medium with both drive wheels under 5% of body weight
    for 25% of ticks. Hopping, not driving.

    The NOSE is a rigid moulded lip that touches down intermittently, and it
    has to STOP the dive rather than absorb it. At the caster's -300 -10 it
    sank 5.5 mm - a 3 mm sphere almost entirely through the floor - and the
    robot kept rotating to 13.9 deg nose-down. Measured, braking from duty 0.7:

      solref        max nose-down    nose penetration
      -300   -10       13.86 deg          5.52 mm
      -3000  -30        8.60 deg          1.71 mm
      -10000 -60        7.74 deg          0.94 mm     <- shipped
      -30000 -100       7.32 deg          0.63 mm

    It has converged by -10000 (zeta ~ 0.73): the lip first touches at 6.3 deg
    and then sinks 1.4 deg further, which is what a hard plastic edge does.
    Note the wheels unload for ~12% of the braking phase at EVERY stiffness, so
    that is the braking dynamics and not something the contact is causing.

    The caster's ROLLING terms (4th/5th) must stay small: that is what lets the
    ball roll rather than skid. Its sliding mu is deliberately HIGH (0.4) for
    the same reason - a ball caster needs grip to roll. The nose skid is the
    opposite: it only ever slides, and (B) reports that graze as low friction.
  -->
  <contact>
    <pair name="floor_caster" geom1="floor" geom2="caster_geom"
          friction="0.35 0.35 0.001 0.0001 0.0001"
          solref="-5000 -40"/>
    <pair name="floor_nose" geom1="floor" geom2="nose_skid"
          friction="0.35 0.35 0.001 0.0001 0.0001"
          solref="-10000 -60"/>
  </contact>

  <actuator>
    <!-- kv = T_stall/omega_max = 0.0368/230 = 1.6000e-4; ctrlrange = +-1.2*omega_max -->
    <velocity name="left_motor"  joint="left_wheel_joint"  kv="1.6e-4" ctrlrange="-276 276"/>
    <velocity name="right_motor" joint="right_wheel_joint" kv="1.6e-4" ctrlrange="-276 276"/>
  </actuator>

</mujoco>
